# Design System & Visual Identity

## Design Philosophy

Vyayamy is a **calm, minimal workout journal**. Every design decision follows this priority order — never invert it:

1. **Clarity** — the user should instantly understand what they're looking at
2. **Usability** — every interaction should feel obvious and forgiving
3. **Speed** — perceived and actual; no layout jank, no unnecessary loading states
4. **Consistency** — identical patterns for identical actions, everywhere
5. **Delight** — subtle motion and refined details, earned only after the above are satisfied

### Foundational Principles

**Dieter Rams — remove everything unnecessary.** Fewer buttons, fewer visual elements, more whitespace, cleaner screens. If an element doesn't directly serve the user's current task, it shouldn't be on screen.

**Don Norman — match human mental models.** Behavior must be predictable. Labels must be clear. Actions must be obvious. Never make the user guess what a tap will do.

**Alan Kay — powerful systems should still be understandable.** Complexity is introduced progressively. Patterns are modular and consistent. A new user and a power user should both feel at home.

**Alan Dye / Matias Duarte — motion and visual systems create personality.** Smooth transitions, a cohesive design token system, and refined micro-details give the app its character.

### Visual Direction

Lean toward **Apple Health / Apple Fitness** aesthetics — not typical fitness app design. The inspiration stack is Apple Health, Apple Fitness, Whoop, Strava, Strong, and Hevy, but the visual execution stays closer to Apple: restrained, typographic, spacious.

### Non-negotiables

- **Single-column layout**, max-width 640px, centered — this is a phone-first app
- **Warm neutral palette** — stone/amber tones (`#F8F8F6` bg, `#1C1917` text). No saturated brand colors, no dark mode (unless explicitly requested)
- **System font stack** (`-apple-system`, `SF Pro`, `system-ui`) — native feel, zero web-font overhead
- **44px minimum touch targets** on all interactive elements (`--touch-min`)
- **Generous whitespace** — when in doubt, add more breathing room, not less
- **Subtle, purposeful animation** — page-in fades (`translateY(8px)`), sheet slides, scale-on-press (`scale(0.98)`). No bouncy/spring physics, no particle effects, no gratuitous motion
- **Progressive disclosure** — show the essential action first; reveal advanced options only when the user reaches for them

## CSS Custom Properties

All values come from `src/styles/theme.css`. Never hard-code colors, spacing, radii, shadows, or font sizes — always reference tokens:

```css
/* Correct */
padding: var(--space-4);
color: var(--color-text-secondary);
border-radius: var(--radius-md);

/* Wrong */
padding: 16px;
color: #78716C;
border-radius: 12px;
```

## CSS Architecture

- **One CSS file per component**, co-located: `ComponentName.tsx` + `ComponentName.css`
- **No CSS modules, no CSS-in-JS, no utility classes** — plain `.css` files imported in the component
- Global utility classes (`.btn-primary`, `.btn-secondary`, `.btn-ghost`, `.page-title`, `.section-title`, `.card-title`, `.meta`, `.tabular`) live in `theme.css` — reuse them

## Class Naming

BEM-inspired flat naming:

| Pattern                | Example                          |
|------------------------|----------------------------------|
| Block                  | `.exercise-block`                |
| Block + element        | `.exercise-block-head`           |
| Block + element + part | `.exercise-block-head-text`      |
| Modifier               | `.set-row--done`, `.toast--info` |

Never nest selectors deeper than two levels. Keep specificity low.

## Animations

Scope `@keyframes` to the component CSS file. Use theme transition tokens:

```css
transition: opacity var(--duration-fast) ease;
animation: page-in var(--duration-normal) var(--ease-out);
```

## Responsive

The only breakpoint is `768px` for minor desktop padding/font adjustments. Do not add complex responsive layouts — the app is single-column by design.
